            <div xml:id="d266">

                <pb facs="0382" n="287" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg287"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/8130: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Canton</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Linnell</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Canton</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-09-10T15:00:00+08:00">September 10, 1936—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 10—1:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Regarding Pakhoi incident, my telegram September 8, 11 a.m.,<anchor n="96"
                        xml:id="d266-f1"/> it is now learned that the killing of a Japanese occurred
                    on the border <pb facs="0383" n="288" xml:id="pg288"/> on September 3rd. The
                    Japanese gunboat <hi rendition="#italic">Saga</hi> has sailed from Canton for
                    Pakhoi carrying two officials of the Canton Japanese Consulate General who will
                    investigate case. Dr. Tyau, special delegate for Executive Department’s Foreign
                    Affairs states he advised Japanese Consul in charge not to send delegate to
                    Pakhoi for fear of possible mob incidents but they proceeded nevertheless. Tyau
                    has sent his assistant to Pakhoi to make an investigation. The Japanese Consul
                    in charge states that they will investigate especially whether the alleged
                    position was given out by 19th Route Army forces while carrying on anti-Japanese
                    demonstration. If this is found to be the case Japanese attitude will become
                    much firmer.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, Peiping, Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Linnell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
